 ## Operating characteristics
-**Time and Memory Complexity:**
-*   **Time:** The visibility prior computation requires warping images for multiple depth planes, which adds computational overhead per training iteration. The complexity depends on the number of depth planes $k$ and the image resolution.
-*   **Memory:** Requires storing the plane scan volume, which scales with the number of depth planes and image resolution.
-
-**Measured Performance:**
-*   On the RealEstate-10K dataset, ViP-NeRF achieves a PSNR index 3–4 points higher than the latest paper in 2, 3, and 4 input views [S1].
-
-**Strengths and Limitations:**
-*   **Strengths:** Effective in sparse view scenarios; does not require pre-training; improves density estimation reliability [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Sensitive to the choice of threshold $\tau$ and number of depth planes; may struggle with textureless or transparent regions [S1].
-
-**When to Use:**
-*   Use when training data is sparse (fewer than 50 views) and pre-trained geometric priors are unavailable [S1].
-
-**When Not to Use:**
-*   Avoid when temporal consistency is required (e.g., video synthesis) due to potential artifacts from visibility estimation errors [S2].
+*   **Time and Memory Complexity:** The method requires computing planar scanned volumes for each main-secondary view pair during training, which adds computational overhead. The complexity depends on the number of depth planes and the resolution of the images.
+*   **Measured Performance:** On the RealEstate-10K dataset, ViP-NeRF achieves a PSNR index 3-4 points higher than the latest paper in 2, 3, and 4 input views [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Improves rendering quality under sparse view conditions without pre-training.
+*   **Limitations:** Requires careful tuning of the threshold $\tau$ and the set of depth planes. Performance may degrade if camera poses are inaccurate.
+*   **When to use:** When training NeRFs with very few input views (e.g., 2-4 views) and no external geometric priors are available.
+*   **When not to use:** When temporal consistency is required for video rendering, as related visibility-based interpolation methods are prone to temporal inconsistency [S2].
 
 ## Background
-ViP-NeRF was introduced to address the challenge of novel view synthesis with sparse input views [S1]. It builds on the idea that visibility information between views can provide a reliable estimate to supervise the density field [S1]. The method was cited in a 2024 survey on methods and strategies for improving NeRF quality [S1].
+ViP-NeRF was introduced to address the challenge of novel view synthesis with sparse input views. It builds on the idea that visibility information from multiple views can provide a reliable estimate to supervise the density of the NeRF [S1]. The method was proposed in the context of improving the rendering quality of synthesized images from new viewpoints under sparse input view conditions [S1].
 
